      <h3>What this unit is about</h3>
<p>In 399 BCE, a 70-year-old Athenian man named Socrates was put on trial, convicted, and executed. His crimes, according to the city of Athens, were corrupting the youth and failing to honor the city’s gods. His response, according to his student Plato, was to argue that the unexamined life is not worth living — and to refuse every opportunity to escape or soften his sentence.</p>
<p>This unit follows Socrates through three short dialogues, each capturing a different moment in his final weeks. Together they form a single story about what philosophy is, why it matters, and what it might cost you.</p>
<h4>The three dialogues, in order</h4>
<p><strong>The <em>Euthyphro</em></strong> takes place outside the law court, just before Socrates goes inside to hear the charges against him. He runs into a man named Euthyphro, who is about to prosecute his own father for murder — because, Euthyphro insists, it is the pious thing to do. Socrates asks him what piety actually is. The dialogue that follows raises one of the oldest questions in ethics: is something right because a divine authority commands it, or is it commanded because it is already right?</p>
<p><strong>The <em>Apology</em></strong> is Socrates’ defense speech at his trial. (The Greek word <em>apologia</em> means “defense,” not “apology” in the modern sense.) Rather than playing to the jury, Socrates argues that he is Athens’ greatest benefactor — a gadfly sent to sting the city awake. He is convicted and sentenced to death.</p>
<p><strong>The <em>Crito</em></strong> takes place in prison. Socrates’ old friend Crito arrives with money and a plan to help him escape. Socrates refuses. He argues that escaping would be unjust — a betrayal of the agreement any citizen makes by choosing to live under a city’s laws. The dialogue forces a question that political philosophy has wrestled with ever since: when, if ever, is it right to disobey the law?</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Why it matters</p>
<p>These three dialogues are the foundation of Western philosophy not because Socrates had all the answers, but because he had a method for exposing the questions we hadn’t thought to ask. You’ll encounter that method throughout this course — not just in Plato, but in every philosopher who came after him.</p>
</div></div>
<div>
<p>Looking ahead → Unit 2</p>
<p>The central question of the <em>Euthyphro</em> — does morality depend on divine authority, or does it have an independent foundation? — will reappear in a new form when we reach Descartes. Descartes will argue that God is the guarantor of reason itself. But if that’s true, could God have made two plus two equal five? Keep Euthyphro’s dilemma in mind.</p>
</div>
