      <h3>Before you read: watch this first</h3>
<p>This short video introduces the central philosophical problem of the <em>Euthyphro</em> — the divine command theory and the dilemma Socrates poses — before you encounter it in the text. Watch it now, commit to your own position, and then see how Socrates handles the same question.</p>
<div><div class="video-embed"><iframe title="Crash Course Philosophy #33: Divine Command Theory and the Euthyphro Dilemma" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/wRHBwxC8b8I" width="100%" height="100%" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe></div></div>
<p>Crash Course Philosophy #33: “Divine Command Theory” (~10 min). Captions available via YouTube.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Before you continue</p>
<p>The video ends without resolving the dilemma — that’s intentional. Before moving on, take a moment to write down which position you find more compelling, and why. You’ll be asked to revisit this in your reflection.</p>
</div></div>
<h4>What to notice in the video</h4>
<p>— The two “horns” of the dilemma: what does each one commit you to?</p>
<p>— Why Socrates thinks neither horn is satisfactory</p>
<p>— What the dilemma assumes about the relationship between God and morality</p>
