      <h3>Unit readings</h3>
<p>All three dialogues are short — together they amount to fewer pages than a typical novel chapter. Read them in order; each one sets up the next. The links below go to the MIT Classics translations, which are free and openly accessible.</p>
<h4>Primary texts</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Plato, <em>Euthyphro</em></strong> — <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/euthyfro.html" target="_blank">full dialogue</a></li>
<li><strong>Plato, <em>Apology</em></strong> — <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/apology.html" target="_blank">full dialogue</a></li>
<li><strong>Plato, <em>Crito</em></strong> — <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/crito.html" target="_blank">full dialogue</a></li>
</ol>
<h4>A note on reading Plato</h4>
<p>Plato writes in dialogue form — meaning you are reading a scripted conversation, not an essay or a textbook. Socrates almost never states his own view directly. Instead, he asks questions. Your job as a reader is to follow the argument step by step and notice where it succeeds or breaks down.</p>
<p>It helps to read with a pencil. Mark any moment where you think: “Wait — does that actually follow?” Those are exactly the moments worth noting when you write your reflection.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Reading tip</p>
<p>The <em>Euthyphro</em> ends without a definition of piety. Euthyphro walks away. This is not a flaw in the dialogue — it is the point. Ask yourself: what has been accomplished, if anything?</p>
</div></div>
